    {
      "id": "s1",
      "template": "title",
      "motion": "kinetic",
      "narration": "React updates the screen without redrawing the whole page. The trick is the Virtual DOM.",
      "text": {
        "kicker": "React",
        "heading": "How the Virtual DOM works",
        "sub": "why React feels instant",
        "fill": "gradient"
      },
      "durationSec": 5.712
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "template": "diagram",
      "narration": "The real DOM is slow to touch. So React keeps a lightweight copy in memory, a tree of plain JavaScript objects.",
      "text": {
        "heading": "A copy in memory",
        "diagram": {
          "accentIndex": 1,
          "boxes": [
            {
              "title": "Real DOM",
              "items": [
                "slow to touch"
              ]
            },
            {
              "title": "Virtual DOM",
              "items": [
                "plain JS objects",
                "cheap to rebuild"
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationSec": 7.012
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "template": "code",
      "narration": "Here's a counter. Click it, you call setCount. React builds a new virtual tree instead of touching the page.",
      "text": {
        "heading": "An example",
        "windowLabel": "Counter.jsx",
        "footnote": "setCount schedules a re-render, not a full repaint.",
        "code": [
          "function Counter() {",
          "  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);",
          "  return (",
          "    <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>",
          "      Clicked {count} times",
          "    </button>",
          "  );",
          "}"
        ]
      },
      "durationSec": 7.616
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "template": "stat",
      "narration": "Why does this matter? At sixty frames per second you get about sixteen milliseconds per frame. React diffs the trees and writes only what changed, so you stay in budget.",
      "text": {
        "kicker": "One frame",
        "value": "16ms",
        "sub": "your budget at 60fps",
        "fill": "gradient"
      },
      "durationSec": 10.31
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "template": "bullets",
      "narration": "That's the payoff. React batches updates, writes to the real DOM once, and lets you describe the UI instead of mutating it.",
      "text": {
        "kicker": "Takeaway",
        "heading": "Why it's fast",
        "bullets": [
          "batches updates together",
          "writes to the DOM once",
          "declarative, not manual"
        ]
      },
      "durationSec": 8.313
    },
